  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nSunday and Monday\n\nA weak surface high remains in place across the Central and Northern \nPlains early this morning. Winds are light and there is a thin band \nof cirrus streaming across the area. With time this morning, winds \nwill slowly veer to the south, but they may actually go calm at \nnumber of locations. While patchy fog in low lying areas is still \npossible near sunrise, there are a couple factors working against \nit. There is a slightly drier boundary layer after good mixing \non Saturday and light northerly winds, plus being another day \nremoved from rainfall across far eastern Nebraska and western \nIowa. Also, mid-high clouds look to expand across the area \nbetween 4-7 AM CT, which would put a lid on the radiative \ncooling.\n\nStepping upward in the atmosphere, ridging over the \nIntermountain West leaves the local area in a northwest flow \npattern both in the short term and beyond as the pattern looks \nto remain fairly stubborn over the next several days. There is a\nhealthy fetch of moisture off to our west as well with a 1.25+\"\nPWAT contour extending from west Texas to the Canadian border \nacross the High Plains. There has also been expansive convection\nacross the higher terrain overnight. Showers with embedded \nthunder are expected to walk across western and central Nebraska\nthis morning. Instability and deep layer shear are minimal \nacross the area this morning, so even thunder might be hard to \ncome by across eastern Nebraska. Nonetheless, light showers are\nexpected today along with more mid-level cloud cover. This will\nkeep today's temperatures cooler.\n\nIt's sort of a rinse and repeat pattern on Monday with convection \nwrapping around the ridge to our west and working across the area. \nConvective initiation looks likely upstream in SD aided by \npronounced 925-850mb WAA between 1am-10am Monday. MUCAPE is also a \nlittle better, ~500-1000 J/Kg, across northeast NE during this \ntime with values diminishing south and east. A strong storm or \ntwo cannot be ruled out on Monday - especially near the SD/NE \nborder. \n\nTuesday and Beyond\n\nAs previous discussions have mentioned, the northwest flow \npattern will remain in place through the work week with \nperiodic chances for showers and thunderstorms - generally weak \nin nature. It is really tough to pin down precip timing and \namounts in patterns such as these. The forcing for ascent is \nhard to track with not much run-to-run continuity in the model\nguidance, and the forcing is often weak. While shortwaves \nembedded in the flow have the potential to kick of showers, they\nare hard to diagnosis beyond 24 hours. Nonetheless, we will \nhave several more chances to add to our hot start in terms of \nAugust precipitation:\nOmaha - 5.37\" (+2.08)\nLincoln - 4.57\" (+2.31)\nNorfolk - 5.12\" (+2.55)\n\nLooking at 500mb heights from the WPC cluster analysis \n(EPS/GEFS/CMCE) as we head toward the end of August, we still \nmaintain positive height anomalies across most of the central U.S. \nThus, no big cool downs appear to be on the horizon, although the \nridge axis might shift 1-2 states to the west. That'd likely \nmean better chances for dry weather past Day 7.",
